From: gt-be98-buildroot <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] DFS background radar: a narrower standby when the band has
 no second block at the AP's width, the switch at the standby's width, the
 way back to the configured width after the non-occupancy period

hostapd 2.12 (src/ap/dfs.c), enable_background_radar=1, on top of 0004:

1. hostapd_dfs_update_background_chain() chose the background chain only at
   the AP's own width. On a band with one clean block of that width (5 GHz-
   high in an ETSI country at 80 MHz: 100/80; 116/80 holds the weather
   channels, 132/80 needs 144) the pick was the AP's own block, and the
   fallback dfs_downgrade_bandwidth() returned it again (it excludes nothing)
   AND rewrote the running AP's width in its configuration (the next beacon
   update would advertise the narrower width). Now: a block at the AP's
   width, else narrower (80 -> 40), that does not overlap the AP's block, a
   still-to-clear one first, then an already-available one (ready, no CAC);
   the configuration's width is changed only for the search and restored.
   Every piece of a candidate must be enabled (hostapd's DFS_NO_CAC_YET
   range check does not test the disabled flag: it offered 132/80 over the
   disabled 144, silicon run q343), and a (channel, width) the driver refuses
   is excluded and the selection runs again. The chain records its width.
2. hostapd_dfs_start_channel_switch_background() switched at the AP's
   current width with the chain's centre: with a narrower chain, an invalid
   chandef. Now it switches at the chain's width
   (hostapd_dfs_request_channel_switch() keeps the current width until the
   switch is notified).
3. Nothing ever went back to the configured width. Now, while the AP runs
   narrower than configured, the chain clears the configured-width block
   when it is usable again (after its non-occupancy period, re-armed by
   hostapd_dfs_nop_finished()) and hostapd_dfs_complete_cac() switches back
   to it when that CAC completes.

Only an interface with enable_background_radar=1 on a driver declaring
RADAR_BACKGROUND is affected. Applies after 0001-0004. Compile-checked
(test/build-hostapd-confcheck.sh); host-tested with test/hostapd-dfs-bg-test.c
(build-hostapd-dfs-bg-test.sh: the dfs5gh-zw80 scenario on the device's
5 GHz-high channel table); r2 of the 0005 first run on the device in q343.
